From d876ade176f1d6b0780ad1a4cb53eaeae211187a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:37:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS4 dynarmic: stack and thread-local accesses walk from the
 start once one is declined; cache use per core

03.93 on a console: Mario Kart races at 22-24 fps, but fastmem declined 5,000 accesses a second
while loading and 400-1,900 at a race's start, each one a fault and a recompile of its block
(about 150 us), the old code left dead in the cache: two whole-cache flushes in the session, each
followed by everything translated again. The stutters.

Each fastmem access's patch information records what its address is based on
(Optimization::ClassifyAddressBase: the stack pointer, the thread pointer through
A32CoprocGetOneWord or A64GetTPIDRRO, or other, through constant adds and subtracts). A decline of
a stack or thread-pointer access sets the emitter's stack_walks / thread_local_walks; from then on
every block compiled sends all accesses through that base to A32SharedWalkPass, lone ones too
(min_members 1), so none faults first. Host harness, stack pages out of the fastmem arena, 50
functions with a PUSH/POP each: 1,000 fallbacks before, 20 now; results equal. Old build vs new,
every option on: 1,206 random programs (split stack, fastmem) equal.

PS4Perf: each emulated core's code cache free space (after its last translation) and its whole
flushes, for the 30-second line.
